                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_disaggregate:  `<strong>Good — you split the data.</strong> The 3.4 average hid a 4.2 core and a 2.6 at-risk segment. Disaggregating by tenure and group is the precondition for seeing exclusion at all; the average was protecting you from the very people who are leaving.`,
                    coach_r1_headline:      `<strong>You looked at 3.4 and called it a minor tune-up.</strong> But split it: your core is at 4.2 and your newest, most diverse members are at 2.6 — and they are the ones resigning. Whose experience does the average hide, and who pays for missing it?`,
                    coach_r1_onequote:      `<strong>One resignation is a data point; the 2.6 is the pattern.</strong> The high-potential leaver said “never quite fitting in,” and the pulse shows a whole segment feels the same. Treat it as a systemic signal, not a one-off.`,
                    coach_r1_safetyonly:    `<strong>Safety is not the whole story.</strong> Belonging — feeling valued for who you are — is a distinct, measurable driver. Treat it as a soft extra and you will fix who speaks up without fixing who feels they belong, and the leavers keep leaving.`,
                    coach_r1_same:          `<strong>They reinforce each other, but they are not the same.</strong> Belonging is being valued for who you are; psychological safety is being safe to speak up. Collapse them into one number and you lose the lever that explains why your newer members go quiet and then go.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You disaggregated the data, kept belonging and safety distinct, and named the three real mechanisms — dominant-voice meetings, decisions in the after-work channel, and the “we’ve always done it this way” norm. That is exactly the map you need to intervene.`,
                    coach_r1_people:        `<strong>Careful — “it’s a people problem” is the comfortable answer.</strong> When a whole at-risk segment goes quiet and leaves, that is structure and norms, not careless individuals. Name the mechanisms — meetings, channels, norms — not the people.`,
                    coach_r1_event:         `<strong>A morale event will not move a 2.6.</strong> There absolutely are structural mechanisms here: who talks in meetings, where decisions really get made, and the norm that closes the door. Belonging is built through team design, not a one-off lift in mood.`,
                    coach_r1_lowrigour:     `<strong>Low rigour means you will act on the 3.4.</strong> If you are not willing to read past the comfortable average to the 2.6 experience, you will design for the core who are already fine — and miss the people walking out the door.`,
                    coach_r2_airtime:       `<strong>Restructuring airtime is the high-leverage move.</strong> Round-robins and written-first input quietly change who gets heard — the team recovers the cognitive diversity it hired for, without singling anyone out. Structural beats celebratory almost every time.`,
                    coach_r2_celebration:   `<strong>You planned a “celebrating our diverse team” all-hands and asked the excluded members to share their story.</strong> They did not feel valued — they felt singled out and exposed, and one just declined. What is the difference between including someone and putting them on display?`,
                    coach_r2_email:         `<strong>An email and a poster is not an intervention.</strong> Belonging is built through how meetings run and where decisions get made, not through an announcement on the kitchen wall. This is roughly the inaction the in-group is hoping for.`,
                    coach_r2_channelmiss:   `<strong>Good airtime work — but you left the back channel alone.</strong> If the real decisions still migrate to an after-work thread the excluded members are not in, you have fixed the meeting and lost the decision. Bring decisions into a transparent forum.`,
                    coach_r2_ban:           `<strong>Banning the channel treats the symptom, not the cause.</strong> You cannot forbid people from socialising — and trying to reads as controlling. The fix is to make sure real <em>decisions</em> happen in the open, not to police where colleagues chat.`,
                    coach_r2_token:         `<strong>Appointing a single “diversity champion” is textbook tokenism.</strong> You have made one person responsible for everyone’s inclusion and put them on display, without redistributing any voice or power. That deepens othering rather than fixing it.`,
                    coach_r2_nostruct:      `<strong>No new structures means no lasting change.</strong> Belonging for newer members comes from buddies, sponsorship and co-created norms that outlast your attention. “Let it settle” is how the gap quietly reopens.`,
                    coach_r2_noinvest:      `<strong>You are asking for change on a shoestring.</strong> Structural inclusion work — facilitation, redesigning meetings, onboarding — takes real leader time. Underfund it and it stays a good intention rather than a changed practice.`,
                    coach_r3_nomodel:       `<strong>Your plan has buddies and new formats — all delegated.</strong> But in the data, <em>you</em> interrupt newer members and reward the loudest voice. Edmondson is clear: safety is set by what the leader models. What will you do differently in the very next stand-up?`,
                    coach_r3_repair:        `<strong>That is the loudest right signal you can send.</strong> Acknowledging the incident, repairing with the two members, and visibly changing how the decision is made tells the whole squad that being talked over and left out carries a consequence — and a change.`,
                    coach_r3_smooth:        `<strong>You had a quiet word and moved on.</strong> The rest of the squad is watching how you handle the first real test. If being talked over and left out carries no consequence and no change, what have you just taught everyone is acceptable here?`,
                    coach_r3_ignore:        `<strong>Saying nothing is itself a message.</strong> You have told the team that exclusion is fine as long as the release is in flight — and your QA lead is one of the excluded two. Silence on the incident is the loudest endorsement of the closed door.`,
                    coach_r3_coach:         `<strong>Private coaching, paired with repairing the incident, is exactly right.</strong> You name the behaviour and its impact and enlist the senior engineer in resetting the norm — changing the behaviour without modelling the public humiliation you are trying to eliminate.`,
                    coach_r3_public:        `<strong>You called the senior engineer out in front of the team to make an example.</strong> Their behaviour was a problem — but you just modelled public humiliation, the exact opposite of psychological safety, and now the in-group feels unsafe too. Change the behaviour without destroying the safety you are building.`,
                    coach_r3_avoid:         `<strong>Avoiding the senior engineer because they are a top performer is the trap.</strong> Their “we’ve always done it this way” style is the tone the in-group reads as a closed door. Leave it unaddressed and your structural changes are fighting a current the leader will not name.`,
                    coach_r3_park:          `<strong>You agreed to park belonging until after the release.</strong> But two flight-risk engineers are on that release, idea-sharing from newer members is near zero, and your QA lead is one of the excluded two. If they disengage or leave mid-sprint, does the release ship faster — or not at all?`,
                    coach_r3_either:        `<strong>A token gesture now and the real work later is the worst of both.</strong> The team reads the gesture as performative and the delay as the usual “soft stuff can wait.” Belonging under deadline pressure is precisely when modelling it counts.`,
                    coach_r4_leading:       `<strong>Leading indicators are the right spine.</strong> Airtime distribution, idea-share from newer members, 1:1 sentiment and flight-risk signals tell you whether belonging is rising <em>within</em> the quarter — long before the next pulse confirms it or a resignation does.`,
                    coach_r4_lagging:       `<strong>Waiting for the next pulse is too slow.</strong> A quarterly lagging score will not warn you when idea-share from newer members is sliding. By the time it drops, another flight-risk engineer may already be drafting a resignation.`,
                    coach_r4_nomeasure:     `<strong>“The team feels better” is not a measurement.</strong> Without leading indicators you cannot tell genuine, durable change from a temporary lift in mood — and you cannot defend it to a sceptical People Director.`,
                    coach_r4_campaign:      `<strong>A one-off campaign with a launch event is the classic decay pattern.</strong> The energy fades the moment attention moves on, and the squad slides back to the closed door. Belonging has to be embedded in how the team works, not launched.`,
                    coach_r4_leaderonly:    `<strong>If it all depends on your personal vigilance, it is not embedded.</strong> The day you are pulled onto the next fire, the norms lapse. Build inclusion into the team’s system — formats, onboarding, handover — so it survives without you watching.`,
                    coach_r4_evidence:      `<strong>That is a board-ready, honest case.</strong> You showed the leading indicators already moving, embedded the norms in the system, and framed belonging as what protects the £3.5M release — not what competes with it. That is a plan that survives the quarter.`,
                    coach_r4_optics:        `<strong>“Trust us, the numbers will follow” will not survive the senior engineer.</strong> Optimistic optics without leading-indicator evidence reads as the soft stuff he already doubts. Show the data that is moving, not the mood.`,
                    coach_r4_defensive:     `<strong>“Deadlines are not my problem” loses the room.</strong> Inclusion being right does not exempt you from delivery. The winning case is that belonging is delivery infrastructure — make that argument, not a moral one that ignores the release.`,

