                    cm_m_attrition:         `Attrition risk`,
                    insight_built:          `Belonging built in: you read past the 3.4 to the 2.6, redistributed voice structurally rather than performing diversity, handled the incident as the loudest signal you could send, and modelled the safety yourself. Belonging for newer members is above target and the flight risk is contained — and it survives because it is in the team’s system, not your vigilance.`,
                    insight_tokenism:       `You hit the numbers, but a performative move along the way singled people out. Tokenism can lift a headline while deepening othering underneath — genuine inclusion redistributes voice and power, it does not put people on display.`,
                    insight_thin:           `You avoided the escalation, but the inclusion is thin. Without higher psychological safety and embedded norms, the belonging gain is fragile and likely to decay once the pressure of the release passes.`,
                    insight_short:          `You fell short. Belonging for newer members is still below 3.5 or regretted attrition is still above 12%, so two more flight-risk engineers are expected to leave — putting the £3.5M release and the squad’s credibility at risk. Revisit which mechanism you left unaddressed.`,
                    insight_incident:       `The exclusion incident was never genuinely resolved. The squad read your handling as the real signal of what is acceptable — and concluded that being talked over and left out carries no consequence.`,
                    insight_attrition:      `Regretted-attrition risk is still above the 12% threshold. The newer members who feel they do not belong are exactly the ones the cost-to-replace (~£70k each) and the roadmap can least afford to lose.`,
